    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/james-monroe"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-monroe.jpg" alt="James Monroe" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/james-monroe">James Monroe</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:James_Monroe_White_House_portrait_1819_(cropped)(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Samuel Finley Breese Morse, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Liberia grew out of the work of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-colonization-society">American Colonization Society</a>, founded in 1816 by a coalition of white philanthropists, clergy and slaveholders who wished to resettle free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. After a failed first attempt on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sherbro-island">Sherbro Island</a> in 1820, the society’s agents acquired land at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-mesurado">Cape Mesurado</a> in December 1821, where from 1822 the settlement later named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monrovia">Monrovia</a>, after President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-monroe">James Monroe</a>, was established. Under agents such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehudi-ashmun">Jehudi Ashmun</a> the colony survived disease and conflict with neighbouring peoples, and it was joined by other settlements founded by state colonisation societies, including Maryland in Liberia at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-palmas">Cape Palmas</a>. Alongside free-born and formerly enslaved African Americans, the colony absorbed thousands of “recaptives”, Africans freed from slave ships by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-navy">United States Navy</a> and known locally as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-people-liberia">Congoes</a>. Because the society was a private body, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> refused to recognise its authority to levy customs duties, and this dispute pushed the settlers towards independence. On 26 July 1847 a convention declared the Republic of Liberia, adopting a constitution modelled on that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and the motto “The love of liberty brought us here”. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-jenkins-roberts">Joseph Jenkins Roberts</a>, a merchant born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, became the first president. Britain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> recognised the republic in the following years, but the United States did so only in 1862.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/harbel"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harbel.jpg" alt="Harbel" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/harbel">Harbel</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ASC_Leiden_-_F._van_der_Kraaij_Collection_-_14_-_37_-_A_dirt_road_near_the_Firestone_plantation._Square_houses_of_corrugated_iron_-_Harbel,_Margibi_County_(then_Marshall_County),_Liberia_-_1977-1978.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fred van der Kraaij, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Liberian society was long divided between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americo-liberians">Americo-Liberian</a> settlers and their descendants, a few per cent of the population concentrated in coastal towns, and the indigenous majority, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kpelle-people">Kpelle</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bassa-people-liberia">Bassa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grebo-people">Grebo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kru-people">Kru</a>, Vai, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gola-people">Gola</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kissi-people">Kissi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loma-people">Loma</a>, Mano, Dan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandinka">Mandinka</a>. Settlers built a society modelled on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-south">American South</a>, with Protestant churches, Masonic lodges and two-storey houses, and many indigenous children were raised in settler households as wards. In the nineteenth century the economy depended on coastal trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a>, camwood and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, and on settler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and coffee farms along the St Paul river, which declined with competition from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and elsewhere. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firestone-tire-and-rubber-company">Firestone Tire and Rubber Company</a>’s concession of 1926 created one of the world’s largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> plantations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harbel">Harbel</a>, and rubber remained the leading export until iron ore mining began at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bomi-hills">Bomi Hills</a> in the 1950s, followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bong-mines">Bong</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimba-range">Nimba</a> mines. A ship registry founded in 1948 made Liberia one of the largest flags of convenience in the world. The country has used the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> dollar alongside its own currency. The Vai script, an indigenous syllabary devised in the 1830s, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poro">Poro</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sande-society">Sande</a> societies of the interior reflect the rich cultures of the indigenous peoples.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-world-war.jpg" alt="First World War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bataille_de_Verdun_1916.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Collection DocAnciens/docpix.fr, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For most of its history Liberia relied on militia and a small army rather than on military strength to survive. Settler militias fought repeated wars against indigenous peoples in the nineteenth century, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grebo-people">Grebo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kru-people">Kru</a> of the southeastern coast, and the Liberian Frontier Force, created in 1908 under American and British officers, extended government authority into the interior, often brutally. Liberia joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> on the Allied side in 1917, and in 1918 a German submarine shelled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monrovia">Monrovia</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roberts-international-airport">Roberts Field</a> airport and a port at Monrovia, and Liberia declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1944. The army’s role changed dramatically on 12 April 1980, when Master Sergeant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-doe">Samuel Doe</a> and other soldiers overthrew and killed President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-tolbert">William Tolbert</a>. In December 1989 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-taylor-liberia">Charles Taylor</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-patriotic-front-of-liberia">National Patriotic Front of Liberia</a> invaded from Côte d’Ivoire, starting a civil war in which factions fought largely along ethnic lines and used child soldiers. A West African intervention force, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecomog">ECOMOG</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, entered Monrovia in 1990. A second war from 1999, waged by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberians-united-for-reconciliation-and-democracy">LURD</a> and MODEL rebel groups, ended in 2003. The two wars killed an estimated 250,000 people and displaced over a million, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> mission, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-mission-in-liberia">UNMIL</a>, kept the peace until 2018.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-d-b-king"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-d-b-king.jpg" alt="Charles D. B. King" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-d-b-king">Charles D. B. King</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1927_Charles_Dunbar_Burgess_King.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Auguste Léon, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The constitution of 1847 created a presidency, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bicameral-legislature">bicameral legislature</a> and a supreme court, and limited citizenship to people of African descent, a provision that remains in force. In practice power was held by a narrow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americo-liberians">Americo-Liberian</a> elite. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/true-whig-party">True Whig Party</a> governed without interruption from 1878 to 1980, making Liberia for much of the twentieth century a one-party state, and indigenous people were long excluded from citizenship, voting and office. The interior was ruled indirectly through chiefs under district commissioners, and forced labour and taxation there were frequent sources of abuse. In 1930 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> commission found that officials had shipped labourers to the Spanish island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bioko">Fernando Po</a> under conditions resembling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, and President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-d-b-king">Charles D. B. King</a> and Vice-President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allen-yancy">Allen Yancy</a> resigned. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edwin-barclay">Edwin Barclay</a> (1930–1944) resisted proposals for international trusteeship. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-tubman">William Tubman</a> (1944–1971) pursued an Open Door Policy towards foreign investment and a Unification Policy that extended voting rights and representation to the interior, while maintaining personal control through patronage and security services. After the civil wars a transitional government led to elections in 2005, which made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellen-johnson-sirleaf">Ellen Johnson Sirleaf</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>’s first elected female head of state. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-weah">George Weah</a> was elected in 2017, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-boakai">Joseph Boakai</a> defeated him in 2023.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nuremberg.jpg" alt="Nuremberg" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:N%C3%BCrnberger_Burg_im_Herbst_2013.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DALIBRI, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Liberia holds a distinctive place in African and Atlantic history. Founded as a refuge for formerly enslaved people from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, it became, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, one of only two African states to remain independent during the European partition of the continent, and it was a founding member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organisation-of-african-unity">Organisation of African Unity</a>. Its early statesmen and scholars, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-wilmot-blyden">Edward Wilmot Blyden</a>, who taught at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-liberia">Liberia College</a> and wrote on African identity, helped shape <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-garvey">Marcus Garvey</a>’s movement looked to Liberia in the early 1920s as a possible <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a>. At the same time the republic’s treatment of indigenous peoples, the forced labour scandal of 1930 and the dominance of foreign concessions such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firestone-tire-and-rubber-company">Firestone</a> made it a case study in how independence could coexist with deep inequality and dependency. The civil wars of 1989–2003 destabilised the whole region, spreading into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>, and the conviction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-taylor-liberia">Charles Taylor</a> in 2012 by the Special Court for Sierra Leone was the first conviction of a former head of state by an international tribunal since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a>. Liberian women’s peace activism, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leymah-gbowee">Leymah Gbowee</a>, helped end the second war, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellen-johnson-sirleaf">Johnson Sirleaf</a> and Gbowee shared the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 2011.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/samuel-doe"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samuel-doe.jpg" alt="Samuel Doe" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/samuel-doe">Samuel Doe</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samuel_Kanyon_Doe_-_Liberian.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Photographer: Frank Hall, Treatment: Frank C. Müller, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Liberia’s independence was repeatedly threatened from outside. It lost territory claimed on its northwest and east to British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a> and French colonies in agreements of 1885, 1892 and the early 1900s, and debts from a ruinous loan of 1871 led to an American-supervised customs receivership under a loan of 1912. The settler order ended violently in 1980, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-doe">Samuel Doe</a>’s coup brought the first indigenous Liberian to power and thirteen senior officials of the old regime were publicly executed. Doe’s rule became increasingly repressive and ethnically partisan, and the rigged election of 1985 deepened divisions. The civil war that began in 1989 destroyed the state: Doe was captured and killed in 1990, rival factions controlled much of the country, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-taylor-liberia">Taylor</a>, elected president in 1997, faced a renewed insurgency until he resigned and went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> in August 2003, a week before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accra">Accra</a> peace agreement was signed. Reconstruction under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellen-johnson-sirleaf">Johnson Sirleaf</a> from 2006 brought debt relief and renewed investment, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-african-ebola-epidemic">Ebola epidemic</a> of 2014–2015 killed several thousand Liberians and strained a weak health system. Today Liberia is a fragile multi-party democracy of about five million people that has held peaceful transfers of power, though it remains one of the poorest countries in the world.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Liberia, founded by African American settlers and declared a republic in 1847, is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>’s oldest republic and one of only two African states never colonised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, yet its long domination by a settler elite, its dependence on foreign concessions and the civil wars of 1989–2003 shaped a troubled modern history.</em></p>

